WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.435
-You know my first guest
from her work

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:03.836
in "Crazy Rich Asians"
and "The Farewell."

00:00:03.836 --> 00:00:06.539
Now, she's voicing a character
in the new Disney movie,

00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:08.441
"Raya and the Last Dragon,"

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:11.210
which is in theaters and
available with Premier Access

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:13.479
on Disney+ starting March 5th.

00:00:13.479 --> 00:00:15.914
Here is Awkwafina!

00:00:15.914 --> 00:00:18.350
Ah!
-Hey, hey!

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:19.485
-It's so great to see you.

00:00:19.485 --> 00:00:21.454
Thank you so much
for doing this.

00:00:21.454 --> 00:00:24.123
You have a lot going on
that I want to talk about.

00:00:24.123 --> 00:00:27.093
First off, you were
born and raised in Queens,

00:00:27.093 --> 00:00:30.563
but you spent most of
the past year on the other side

00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:32.131
of the world in Australia.
-Yes.

00:00:32.131 --> 00:00:35.101
-You were out there working on
a new Marvel movie.

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:37.636
-Yes.
-Now what was it like,

00:00:37.636 --> 00:00:39.638
first of all,
being in a foreign country

00:00:39.638 --> 00:00:43.776
in the middle of
a global pandemic?

00:00:43.776 --> 00:00:46.713
-A little discombobulating,
I think.

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:49.649
You know, the way that they did
quarantine there is, like,

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:51.817
you know, they bring you
meals and stuff,

00:00:51.817 --> 00:00:55.121
and there would be a nurse
that would call and, you know,

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:57.690
for, like, I guess mental-health
checkups and she would call

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:58.958
and be like,
"How's your day going,"

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:00.593
and in the beginning I was like,

00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:02.261
"You know, it's fine.
I got to go.

00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:04.397
But then, like, a couple days
go by and I'm just like,

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:05.899
"You know what?
It's not too good."

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:07.500
-"Do you have
a couple minutes here?

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.335
Now that I have you.
Anyway, have you --"

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:12.571
-Let's take advantage
of this service, yeah.

00:01:12.571 --> 00:01:14.774
-"Are you watching any of
the reality shows on Bravo?"

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:17.110
Just start talking to her.

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:18.778
Why not?
-Yeah, it's like "90 Day," man.

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:20.179
-"I need company, I need people,

00:01:20.179 --> 00:01:22.081
I need friends,
I need something."

00:01:22.081 --> 00:01:24.217
You're finally back home to
New York City

00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:26.553
for the first time.
-Yes.

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:30.556
-Is that the longest you've
ever been away from New York?

00:01:30.556 --> 00:01:33.593
-This is the longest I've
ever been away, yeah, for sure.

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:35.728
-I know you also missed
your grandmother.

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:39.432
She still lives in New York, and
you called her your best friend.

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:41.367
I was just curious.

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:45.738
Does she also consider you
to be her best friend?

00:01:45.738 --> 00:01:48.641
-I think she considers a lot of
people to be her best friend.

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:52.611
I think she's definitely told
the delivery guy, you know,

00:01:52.611 --> 00:01:54.614
"You're my best friend."
-No.

00:01:54.614 --> 00:01:57.149
-Yeah, she's loose with it.
She's loose with it.

00:01:57.149 --> 00:01:59.285
She's one of those people that
she'll just call any friend --

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:00.720
-She just throws it out there?

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.522
-Yeah, I don't know
if that's like a best friend.

00:02:02.522 --> 00:02:03.989
I feel like more time,
and, you know,

00:02:03.989 --> 00:02:06.025
maybe, like, you know, maybe,
like, your granddaughter.

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:07.860
Maybe that could be
like a best friendship.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.762
-I want to be her best friend.

00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:11.296
-You could definitely be
her best friend.

00:02:11.296 --> 00:02:14.533
I don't think she would at all
have any reservations,

00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:16.369
you especially.
-Hi, Grandma.

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:19.238
Call me. I'm down to be besties.

00:02:19.238 --> 00:02:21.841
You got to do something
very cool recently,

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:24.544
an honor for any New Yorker.

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:26.546
You became the official voice of

00:02:26.546 --> 00:02:29.381
the 7 train here
in New York City for a while.

00:02:29.381 --> 00:02:32.518
That had to be exciting.

00:02:32.518 --> 00:02:35.521
You also put your own spin
on the subway announcements.

00:02:35.521 --> 00:02:37.123
-Oh. Yes.
-I'll show you what I mean.

00:02:37.123 --> 00:02:38.725
Here's one of them.

00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:41.927
-This is Vernon Boulevard,
Jackson Avenue.

00:02:41.927 --> 00:02:44.564
Hey, fellas, stop manspreading.

00:02:44.564 --> 00:02:46.232
Thanks and have a great day.

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:47.800
-Come on.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.502
We have one more.
Here's another one.

00:02:49.502 --> 00:02:53.505
-This is 82nd Street, Jackson
Heights, and please remember,

00:02:53.505 --> 00:02:57.377
a train car is the worst place
to clip your toenails.

00:02:57.377 --> 00:02:58.811
[ Laughter ]

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:02.581
-Wait. First of all, how --
how exci-- This is a major deal.

00:03:02.581 --> 00:03:05.151
-Yeah, yeah.
I mean, that was awesome.

00:03:05.151 --> 00:03:07.553
I think it was the first time

00:03:07.553 --> 00:03:10.390
the MTA had someone, like,
voice the announcements

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:13.760
in all MTA history, I think.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.662
-What kind of reaction
did you get from people?

00:03:16.662 --> 00:03:19.799
-Well, I think, the first
couple weeks it was, like,

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:21.267
a lot of excitement,
especially for people

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:22.902
who actually knew who I was.

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:25.638
You'd be like, "Oh, my God!
Awkwafina's on the train."

00:03:25.638 --> 00:03:27.973
And then I think it devolved
over a couple weeks,

00:03:27.973 --> 00:03:31.444
especially during, like,
peak rush hour in the morning

00:03:31.444 --> 00:03:33.746
and the night --
I mean, you know, the evening.

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:37.316
I'd get a lot of like, you know,
"No one asked for this."

00:03:37.316 --> 00:03:40.252
And it's like, "This is
the last thing I want to hear

00:03:40.252 --> 00:03:43.455
on, like,
a horrible commute home."

00:03:43.455 --> 00:03:44.990
So eventually, I was just like,

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:48.461
"You know, I'm sorry."
You know, I felt bad.

00:03:48.461 --> 00:03:51.397
-No way! People loved it.
It's funny. It's so cool.

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:53.199
-Good, good.

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:55.434
-I want to talk about
your new Disney movie,

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:57.203
another Disney movie.

00:03:57.203 --> 00:04:00.740
This one's called
"Raya and the Last Dragon."

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.643
Can you set up the movie
and tell us about the character?

00:04:03.643 --> 00:04:07.112
-Yeah, so the movie is
a very special movie

00:04:07.112 --> 00:04:09.082
that I haven't really seen
anything like it.

00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:13.519
It's an adventure
throughout the movie,

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:17.056
throughout this kind of
broken land called Kumandra,

00:04:17.056 --> 00:04:19.558
and there are these universal
truths kind of like

00:04:19.558 --> 00:04:23.095
of trust and unity
and divisiveness that,

00:04:23.095 --> 00:04:25.030
since the very beginning,
since I was involved,

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:28.500
always seemed to kind of ring
true, and obviously, Raya,

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:31.637
our first Southeast Asian
Disney Princess,

00:04:31.637 --> 00:04:33.038
voiced by Kelly Marie Tran,

00:04:33.038 --> 00:04:35.107
and I play the last dragon,
Sisu.

00:04:35.107 --> 00:04:39.945
And we kind of team up
to go on this journey

00:04:39.945 --> 00:04:41.881
throughout Kumandra
to try and fix it.

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:44.550
-You're also starring in another
Disney movie very soon --

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:45.952
I am so excited about this --

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:47.820
the remake of
"The Little Mermaid."

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.121
-Yeah.

00:04:49.121 --> 00:04:50.756
-You're the voice of Scuttle.

00:04:50.756 --> 00:04:52.625
-Yes. Yes.

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:53.693
-This is bizarre.

00:04:53.693 --> 00:04:55.060
We have a picture of you
as a little kid.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:56.729
Look at this.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:58.031
-Oh, man.

00:04:58.031 --> 00:04:59.598
-First of all,
the cutest thing ever.

00:04:59.598 --> 00:05:02.869
Second of all, you're wearing
a "Little Mermaid" shirt.

00:05:02.869 --> 00:05:04.170
-Yep.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:07.573
-What would you have said
if someone told that kid,

00:05:07.573 --> 00:05:08.907
"Hey, one day
you're gonna actually be

00:05:08.907 --> 00:05:12.311
in 'The Little Mermaid'"?

00:05:12.311 --> 00:05:14.847
-I mean, I think if you went up
to that kid and you were like,

00:05:14.847 --> 00:05:16.415
"Kid, you're gonna be
in 'The Little Mermaid,'

00:05:16.415 --> 00:05:18.718
I think I would have called
the cops, to be honest.

00:05:18.718 --> 00:05:21.153
[ Laughter ]
Like, who does that, you know?

00:05:21.153 --> 00:05:23.589
-Yeah, I guess, yeah.
That's true. That's true.

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:26.558
-Keep it in
for a little while longer.

00:05:26.558 --> 00:05:29.795
-"I'm from the future and you're
gonna be in the movie."

00:05:29.795 --> 00:05:32.064
-"Hey, kid, come here.
Come into this bush."

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:33.398
[ Laughter ]

00:05:33.398 --> 00:05:38.638
-Oh, my God. Bush, oh, my gosh.

00:05:38.638 --> 00:05:42.375
-I think that kid really
would not have believed it.

00:05:42.375 --> 00:05:44.143
-That's so cool.

00:05:44.143 --> 00:05:46.378
-Yeah, it was such an iconic
movie growing up for me.

00:05:46.378 --> 00:05:48.280
You know,
all the Disney movies were.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:51.217
So, you know, to know that I can
be in especially a reprisal

00:05:51.217 --> 00:05:53.986
of my favorite one, yeah, crazy.

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:55.187
-We can't wait for that,

00:05:55.187 --> 00:05:57.056
and congrats on
"Raya and the Last Dragon."

00:05:57.056 --> 00:05:58.891
-Thank you. Thanks.
-Sisu, I love it.

00:05:58.891 --> 00:06:00.392
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:06:00.392 --> 00:06:03.763
Here is Awkwafina as Sisu
in "Raya and the Last Dragon."

00:06:03.763 --> 00:06:04.930
Take a look.

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:06.866
-Oh. Oh, I'm sorry,
I-I didn't see ya there.

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:09.168
[ Laughs ] Not too bad.
Just a little dusty.

00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:10.502
Let me get that for ya.
[ Laughs ]

00:06:10.502 --> 00:06:15.441
-Sisu? Y-You are Sisu?

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:17.776
-And you're people.

00:06:17.776 --> 00:06:18.811
What's your name?

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:20.045
-Raya. I-I'm Raya.

00:06:20.045 --> 00:06:23.615
-And you're not made of stone,
which means --

00:06:23.615 --> 00:06:25.417
-It worked!
-We did it!

00:06:25.417 --> 00:06:27.753
Do you hear that, Peku?
It worked!

00:06:27.753 --> 00:06:29.488
I didn't mess it up!

00:06:29.488 --> 00:06:30.789
Is that food?
-[ Grunts ]

00:06:30.789 --> 00:06:32.325
-I was so focused on
saving the world,

00:06:32.325 --> 00:06:33.825
I forgot to have breakfast
today.

00:06:33.825 --> 00:06:35.428
-Awkwafina, everybody.

00:06:35.428 --> 00:06:38.364
"Raya and the Last Dragon"
is in theaters and available

00:06:38.364 --> 00:06:41.734
on Disney+ with Premier Access
starting March 5th.

